# Saving a response body to disk yourself, via a post-request script and
# the preloaded "ioutil" module (same mechanism 4_shell_auth.http uses for
# "cmd").
#
# response.body is always the raw response text in httpfly, regardless of
# Content-Type -- unlike some other HTTP-file tools, it's never pre-parsed
# into an object for you. That makes saving a JSON/text response as simple
# as writing response.body straight to disk; no re-serialization needed.
#
# This works for binary content too, not just text -- Lua strings are
# byte arrays, not decoded Unicode text, so response.body round-trips
# through ioutil.write_file byte-for-byte (confirmed against this exact
# PNG endpoint: identical MD5 to a direct download). "# @download" (see
# 7_download.http) is still the more convenient way to save a response
# as-is: no script needed, and it works the same from :HttpSend or
# :HttpSendAll. Reach for a script instead when you need to save
# something *derived* from the response (a re-serialized subset, a
# different filename per response value, ...) rather than the body
# verbatim.
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` from the repo root first (requires Docker).

###
# @name SaveJsonResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/get?report=example HTTP/1.1

> {%
  local ioutil = require("ioutil")
  ioutil.write_file("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.json", response.body)
%}

###
# @name SaveTextResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/robots.txt HTTP/1.1

> {%
  require("ioutil").write_file("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.txt", response.body)
%}
